#+TITLE: macOS Editor Direction

* Problem

The SwiftUI rendered block editor has been carrying too much responsibility. It is useful as a structured reading, review, and workflow surface, but trying to make every rendered block behave like a full native text editor has produced fragile cursor, selection, autosave, and inline-editing behavior.

Org2's source of truth is still ordinary text files. The macOS app should reinforce that model instead of creating a second editor semantics layer in SwiftUI.

* North Star

The primary writing experience in the macOS app should feel like a fast native text editor with Org2-aware affordances layered on top:

- Source text remains the authoritative editable buffer.
- The editor should use native text editing behavior for cursor movement, selection, undo, keyboard input, and save semantics.
- Org2 semantics should come from the shared parser/runtime wherever practical.
- Rendered views should stay excellent for reading, reviewing, navigation, approvals, agenda actions, folding, and structured mutations.
- Raw/source editing is the full-fidelity escape hatch and should become the default writing surface until the rendered editor is proven reliable.

* Rendering Architecture

The editor and renderer should be separate concerns:

- Native source editing is the authoring core. It owns cursor behavior, selection, undo, IME, keyboard input, and save semantics.
- The shared Org2 compiler/runtime sits between source text and every rendered surface. It should provide source ranges, IDs, semantic blocks, diagnostics, and export/render data.
- Native SwiftUI remains the right layer for workspace chrome and structured workflow controls such as agenda rows, approvals, TODO/planning/property menus, file navigation, and agent handoff actions.
- HTML is a strong candidate for rich read/preview rendering. Org2 pages, generated artifacts, tables, citations, backlinks, published-style previews, and custom views may be easier to render well through compiler-generated HTML than through hand-built SwiftUI rows.
- HTML/WebView state should not become the authoritative editing state unless we deliberately adopt a proper editor engine and accept that complexity. The default writing path stays native text.

* Short-Term Plan

Status: complete. Source editing is now the normal edit path from rendered views:

- The toolbar Edit command opens the source editor for the current entry or page.
- Editing from a rendered block opens the source editor with the caret near that block's source range.
- Rendered rows select, navigate, fold, and expose actions, but single-click text editing is disabled.
- Direct rendered text editors are disabled for headings, paragraphs, and list items.
- Existing structural commands such as insert, move, delete, TODO changes, planning changes, and property edits continue to mutate source text through the store.
- Keep legacy block editing code available for narrow structural internals and tests while it is being retired from normal UI entry points.

Current source-editor writing-loop coverage:

- Return continues active list markers, checkbox list markers, ordered list numbering, and indentation.
- Tab and Shift-Tab demote/promote headings and indent/outdent list items in the source editor.
- Focused tests cover writing through the source editor and saving the resulting document text.
- Regression tests cover source-editor entry from current scope, agenda/rendered row selection, approvals, page-scope files, direct typing, and rendered-selection replacement.

* Medium-Term Plan

Status: complete. The native source editor is now the first-class writing surface:

- ~OrgSyntaxTextEditor~ preserves native selection and undo behavior, highlights the edited and visible line ranges incrementally, and avoids whole-document tokenization for large buffers.
- Source commands cover heading and list insertion, heading/list promotion and demotion, TODO cycling, scheduling and deadlines, property and link insertion, heading navigation, and presentation-only source folding.
- Commands are available from a compact source toolbar, the macOS Source menu, and Mac-native shortcuts. Vim-style or SpaceMacs-style bindings remain a future optional keymap.
- A debounced editor-analysis process runs the shared parser against the current in-memory buffer. Parser-provided ranges drive navigation, structured commands, folding, subtle semantic region presentation, and inline diagnostics without becoming a second editable model.
- Command edits are applied to the live ~NSTextView~ buffer and participate in native undo. They do not write around unsaved text through store-level file mutations.
- Command-S writes exactly the visible buffer and keeps the editor open with a clean baseline. Background reloads are deferred while source editing is active, and a stale file produces a visible save conflict without discarding the draft.
- Focused regressions cover source-editor entry from agenda and rendered selections, full-page files, semantic analysis, structured source commands, large-buffer highlighting, save continuity, reload deferral, and stale-write conflicts.
- Read mode is compiler-generated HTML in a ~WKWebView~. Entry/page scope, source links, folding, property drawers, block quotes, and published-style typography are handled in the read pipeline while authoring remains native source text.

* Longer-Term Options

After the source editor is solid, richer rendering can return as previews, overlays, and sidecars rather than as the authoritative text input path:

- Gutter controls for TODOs, priorities, scheduling, backlinks, and block actions.
- Inline previews for links, images, source blocks, citations, and generated artifacts.
- Optional split source/HTML preview while editing, still separate from the active text buffer.
- Compiler-generated HTML views for published-style pages, generated artifacts, graph/backlink views, and tables where WebView rendering is a better fit than SwiftUI.
- Selective hybrid widgets only where they do not fight native text editing behavior.
